-- title:
--   ℤ₍ₚ₎ is a principal ideal ring
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a natural number, and let [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt p`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) denote the subring of $\mathbb{Q}$ whose elements are those rationals $q$ whose denominator $q.\mathrm{den}$ (the positive denominator of the reduced form) is coprime to $p$; closure under multiplication, addition and negation and containment of $0$ and $1$ hold because the denominator of a sum or product divides the product of the denominators. The assertion is that this subring, viewed as a commutative ring in its own right, is a principal ideal ring: every ideal of it is generated by a single element. No primality or positivity hypothesis is imposed on $p$; the statement is uniform in $p\in\mathbb{N}$, so it covers in particular $p=0$, where the condition $\gcd(q.\mathrm{den},0)=1$ forces $q.\mathrm{den}=1$ and the ring is $\mathbb{Z}$, the case $p=1$, where the condition is vacuous and the ring is $\mathbb{Q}$, the case of prime $p$, where the ring is the usual local ring $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$, and composite $p$, where it is the semilocal ring of rationals whose denominator is prime to every prime divisor of $p$. Note that the conclusion is the principal-ideal property alone; integrality of the ring is not part of the statement.
--
--   This is the standard fact that $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$, and more generally any localisation of $\mathbb{Z}$ inside $\mathbb{Q}$, has all ideals principal. It supplies the principal-ideal hypothesis for the ring over which flatness at $p$ of a Galois representation is formulated, and is used by the finite-flat and flat base-change results built on that base ring.
